= Knowledge graph =

== Overview ==
A '''knowledge graph''' is a graph-based [[Knowledge representation|knowledge representation]] in which nodes and edges encode entities, concepts, types, relations, facts or other structured knowledge.

Knowledge graphs are used for:
* search;
* data integration;
* recommendation;
* question answering;
* scientific knowledge;
* enterprise data;
* artificial intelligence.

There is no single universally accepted definition.

== Graph structure ==
A graph contains nodes and edges.

A simple knowledge graph might represent:

<pre>
Mars ──instanceOf──> Planet
Mars ──orbits──────> Sun
</pre>

The nodes identify represented things.

The labeled edges express [[Relation|relations]].

Knowledge graphs can also include literal values such as dates, names or measurements.

== Data and schema layers ==
Many knowledge graphs distinguish:

'''data/instance layer''' — specific entities and facts.

'''schema/ontology layer''' — classes, relation types and constraints.

Example:

<pre>
Mars instanceOf Planet
Planet subclassOf CelestialBody
</pre>

A reasoner may then infer:

<pre>
Mars instanceOf CelestialBody
</pre>

Not every knowledge graph has a formal ontology.

== Knowledge graph versus graph database ==
A graph database stores/query graph-shaped data.

A knowledge graph additionally aims to encode meaningful domain knowledge, often using:
* types;
* shared identifiers;
* schema;
* provenance;
* ontology;
* inference;
* entity resolution.

The boundary is fuzzy.

Calling a database a knowledge graph does not automatically make its edges semantically rich.

== Knowledge graph versus ontology ==
An [[Ontology]] defines concepts, relations and axioms.

A knowledge graph contains/asserts data/facts and can use an ontology.

Thus:

<pre>
ontology can govern a knowledge graph
knowledge graph can instantiate an ontology
ontology ≠ knowledge graph
</pre>

Some systems call the combined schema + data graph the knowledge graph.

The scope should be stated.

== RDF knowledge graphs ==
[[Resource Description Framework|RDF]] is commonly used for knowledge graphs.

RDF triples naturally form a directed labeled graph.

RDFS and [[Web Ontology Language|OWL]] add richer schema/ontology semantics.

However, knowledge graphs can also use:
* property graphs;
* custom graph models;
* relational backends;
* document stores.

RDF is common, not mandatory.

== Entity resolution ==
Large knowledge graphs frequently combine sources.

The system must decide when different records refer to the same [[Entity|entity]].

Bad merges create false facts.

Failure to merge duplicates fragments knowledge.

Entity resolution therefore interacts directly with [[Identity|identity]] and [[Provenance|provenance]].

== Provenance ==
A graph assertion may need provenance:

<pre>
source
time
method
confidence
author
version
</pre>

Without provenance, a graph can show a fact without showing why it should be trusted.

This is especially important when facts are extracted automatically.

== Deductive inference ==
A knowledge graph can derive new facts using formal rules.

Example:

<pre>
A subclassOf B
x instanceOf A
→ x instanceOf B
</pre>

This is symbolic inference.

Its validity depends on the semantics of the graph/schema language.

== Inductive completion ==
Knowledge graph systems also use machine learning to predict missing links.

An embedding model might estimate:

<pre>
score(entity1, relation, entity2)
</pre>

A high score suggests a possible edge.

It is not the same as a logically entailed fact.

Predicted edges should preserve their epistemic status and provenance.

== Query ==
Knowledge graphs can support graph-pattern queries.

RDF graphs commonly use SPARQL.

Property graphs use other graph-query languages.

Queries retrieve patterns; they do not necessarily perform ontology inference unless the system's query semantics includes an entailment regime.

== Knowledge graph construction ==
Construction can involve:

* manual curation;
* structured database import;
* entity extraction;
* relation extraction;
* ontology alignment;
* entity resolution;
* data cleaning;
* provenance capture;
* validation.

Automatically extracted graphs can scale rapidly but require quality controls.

== Dynamic knowledge ==
Real-world facts change.

A useful knowledge graph may need:
* time;
* versioning;
* source history;
* retractions;
* temporal validity.

Replacing old facts without history can destroy provenance.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki can be projected as a knowledge graph using pages, SMW properties and derived graph views.

That graph is one application of the general concept, not its definition.

